general_notes.general_notes:
This is a preprint (medRxiv) posted November 7, 2024, not yet peer-reviewed. The study represents the largest OCT-based normative modeling study to date with N=56,545 healthy controls. This is notably NOT a neuroimaging study in the traditional sense (MRI, PET, etc.) but rather uses optical coherence tomography (OCT) of the retina. However, it is relevant to the systematic review because: (1) the retina is embryologically and anatomically an extension of the central nervous system; (2) the study explicitly frames retinal measurements as proxy markers for brain structural changes; (3) the normative modeling methodology and statistical framework are directly analogous to those used in neuroimaging normative modeling studies. The study uses standard GAMLSS normative modeling approach similar to BrainChart and other large-scale neuroimaging studies. Comprehensive robustness analyses included multiple validation approaches. Patient groups were identified using ICD-10 diagnostic codes (F20 for SZ, F31 for BD, F33 for MDD). The study excluded individuals with diagnosed eye disorders (data field 6148) from the healthy control group. Laterality effects were systematically investigated, with main analyses using averaged left-right values but supplementary mixed model analyses confirming results with laterality as repeated measure. Distribution selection for GAMLSS was data-driven through cross-validation, with normal distribution selected as most parsimonious. The study acknowledges theoretical possibility of modeling skewness and kurtosis but found normal distribution sufficient. Code will be made available at https://github.com/homanlab/ after publication. Data available through UK Biobank application process. Funding from NARSAD/Brain & Behavior Research Foundation, Novartis Foundation, ERC Synergy Grant, and Hans and Marianne Schwyn Foundation. PH reports conflicts of interest (grants/honoraria from pharmaceutical companies). This study is part of emerging literature using retina as 'window to the brain' in psychiatric disorders.


