ing chi-squared statistics) of outlier data points in each group (HC, MDD, BD, SZ) was performed which was non-significant for differences in outlier observation rate (all χ2 < 1.86, all p> 0.17).", "page_line": "Lines 40-51"}, "site_effect_handling_eval": {"answer": "NA", "confidence_rating": "High", "negative_answer_category": "Not Negative", "reason": "The UK Biobank is a single large-scale cohort study with standardized OCT acquisition protocol. All OCT images were acquired using a spectral domain optical coherence tomography device with a consistent raster scan protocol. Therefore, traditional site/scanner effects are not a primary concern. However, the study appropriately adjusted for factors that could introduce systematic variability, including image quality and ethnicity.", "supporting_text": "This analysis used cross-sectional measurements of optical coherence tomography (OCT) from the UK Biobank database (Figure 1A). The OCT measurement procedure, including inclusion and exclusion criteria and quality control metrics have been previously described. Briefly, optical coherence tomography images were acquired using a spectral domain optical coherence tomography device, with a raster scan protocol of 6×6mm area centered on the fovea, consisting of 128 B-scans each with 512 A-scans, completed in 3.7 seconds... Image quality and reported ethnic background were also included as covariates, since they have been shown to influence retinal parameters.", "page_line": "Lines 39-45, 60-61"}}
